/// Chapter 21 of the guide, written from md-docs/21-media.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page MediaGuide(path: "/docs/guide/media", title: "Media", description: "Add images from public/ and the image pipeline, Video with captions, Audio with a transcript, the 72 built-in icons, Carousel and Frame.", layout: DocsShell("Media", chapter: t("nav.group.ui"), blurb: "Add pictures the build resizes for every screen, video with captions, audio with a transcript, icons, carousels and frames.", source: "md-docs/21-media.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "add-a-picture-from-public", label: "Add a picture from public/"), Anchor(id: "process-a-picture-with-image", label: "Process a picture with image"), Anchor(id: "tune-the-image-pipeline", label: "Tune the image pipeline"), Anchor(id: "add-video-and-audio", label: "Add video and audio"), Anchor(id: "show-an-icon", label: "Show an icon"), Anchor(id: "show-a-carousel", label: "Show a carousel"), Anchor(id: "show-another-page-in-a-frame", label: "Show another page in a frame"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/motion", prevLabel: t("ch.20"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/accessibility", nextLabel: t("ch.22"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to put pictures, sound, video, icons and other pages on a WebFluent page. The build resizes your pictures for every screen and reserves their space, so the page does not jump while they load.\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- Add a picture from `public/`, and let the build process it.\n- Declare a picture with `image` and read its size and colour.\n- Tune the image pipeline, and see what it will write.\n- Add video with captions and audio with a transcript.\n- Show an icon, a carousel, and another page in a frame.")

        Heading("Add a picture from public/", id: "add-a-picture-from-public").h2

        Markdown("To show a picture, put the file in `public/` and name it in `Image(src:)` with a site-relative path. Files in `public/` are served from the site's root, so `public/hero.jpg` is `/hero.jpg`.\n\nThis page shows a photograph, a decorative picture, a video and a talk:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Media(path: "/") {
    Image(src: "/hero.jpg", alt: "The team at the launch", width: 1200, height: 630)
    Image(src: "/deco.png", alt: "")
    Video(src: "/demo.mp4", captions: "/demo.en.vtt").controls
    Audio(src: "/talk.mp3", transcript: "/talk.txt").controls
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Give every `Image` an `alt:` that says what the picture shows. A decorative picture takes `alt: \"\"`, so a screen reader skips it.\n\nWhen `src:` is a literal path into `public/` that names a PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP file, the build processes the file the same way it processes an [`image` declaration](#process-a-picture-with-image):\n\n- It writes the picture again at each width the page may ask for.\n- It sets `width` and `height` from the file, so nothing on the page shifts when the picture arrives.\n- It serves the picture as a `<picture>` element. `sizes:` and `placeholder:` work here too.\n\nThe build uses an SVG file, a URL, or a path built at run time as it is written. Give those a `width:` and `height:` when you know them. To turn processing off, set `build.media.pipeline` to `false`.\n\nThe first image on a page loads eagerly at high priority, because it is usually the largest thing the reader sees first. Every later image loads lazily. Set `loading:` on an `Image` to choose for yourself.")

        Heading("Process a picture with image", id: "process-a-picture-with-image").h2

        Markdown("To use a picture's real size and colour in your page, declare it with `image`. The declaration hands the build the file itself. The build looks for the path in the project's folder, then in `src/`, then in `public/`.\n\nThis page declares a hero picture, shows it with a blurred placeholder, and prints its size and average colour:")

        CodeBlock(#"image hero = "hero.jpg"

page Home(path: "/", title: "Home", description: "The front page.") {
    Image(hero, alt: "The team at the launch",
          sizes: "(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 1200px",
          placeholder: .blur)
    Text("It is {hero.width} by {hero.height}, and mostly {hero.color}.")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The build reads the picture's real size and average colour, writes it again at every width in `media.widths` that is smaller than the original, and gives the page a `<picture>` with:\n\n- a `<source>` per format, each with a `srcset` of the widths, so the browser picks the file it needs for the space `sizes:` describes;\n- `width` and `height` from the file, so the box has the right shape before any of the picture arrives and **nothing on the page moves**;\n- a placeholder while the picture loads: `.blur` inlines a copy sixteen pixels wide as a data URI, `.color` fills the box with the average colour, and `.none` does neither;\n- a content hash in every file's name, so a host can cache each file forever.\n\nThe name `hero` is also a value. You can read `hero.src`, `hero.width`, `hero.height`, `hero.color`, `hero.placeholder` and `hero.srcset`.\n\nThe attributes any element takes (`id:`, `aria-*`, `data-*`, `ref:`, an `on load` handler) go on the `<img>` inside the `<picture>`. That is where its `alt:` is and where `load` fires. The width, the height and the sources stay the build's.")

        Heading("Tune the image pipeline", id: "tune-the-image-pipeline").h2

        Markdown("To change which formats and widths the build writes, set `build.media` in `webfluent.app.json`. These are the defaults:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "build": { "media": {
    "formats": ["webp"],
    "widths": [480, 960, 1440, 1920],
    "quality": 78,
    "pipeline": true
} } }"#, title: "json")

        Table(caption: "Tune the image pipeline") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Key").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`formats`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The formats each picture is written in, best first: `webp`, `png`, `jpeg`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`widths`") }
                    Table.Cell("The widths written. A width larger than the original is skipped; the build never scales a picture up")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`quality`") }
                    Table.Cell("The quality of a lossy file. WebP and JPEG are written lossily at this quality; PNG is written losslessly")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`pipeline`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`false` copies each file as it is, as `public/` files always were") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("The build writes a file only when it saves something. A file that would be no smaller than the original is not written: the original, which the `<img>` falls back to, serves at that width instead. A format that has no width left gets no `<source>`. A JPEG that was already saved small often ends up this way.\n\nThe build caches its work in `.wf-cache/media/`, so a build that changes no image does no image work. A PDF or a slide deck embeds the real picture rather than drawing an empty box.\n\nTo see what the pipeline will make of a picture before it does it, run `wf assets --plan`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf assets --plan /hero.jpg")
            ])

        Markdown("It prints each width and format, what each file comes to, and the widths it will not write because they save nothing. `wf assets` with no option lists every file in `public/` with where it is used, and counts the files nothing uses.")

        Heading("Add video and audio", id: "add-video-and-audio").h2

        Markdown("To show a video, use `Video(src:)`; to play sound, use `Audio(src:)`. Add the `.controls` flag to show the browser's playback controls.\n\nGive every video captions and every recording a transcript:\n\n- `captions:` names a WebVTT file. The build writes it as a `<track>`, so a reader who cannot hear the video can follow it.\n- `transcript:` names a text file. The build draws it as a link beneath the player.\n\nThe first example in this chapter shows both. A `Video` without `captions:`, and an `Audio` without `transcript:`, each draw an `A09` warning.")

        Heading("Show an icon", id: "show-an-icon").h2

        Markdown("To draw an icon, write `Icon(\"name\")`. The icon is an SVG drawn inline in the current text colour, so it follows the theme. The `.sm` and `.lg` flags size it, and a tone flag such as `.primary` colours it.\n\nThis page shows icons on their own, on an icon-only button, and in a sidebar:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Inbox(path: "/", title: "Inbox", description: "Messages, with icons.") {
    Heading("Inbox").h1
    Row(gap: .sm) {
        Icon("mail")
        Icon("bell").lg.primary
        IconButton(icon: "close", label: "Close the inbox")
    }
    Sidebar {
        Sidebar.Item(to: "/", icon: "home") { Text("Home") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`IconButton` is a button that shows only an icon. Its `label:` is its accessible name for screen readers, and also its tooltip unless you give it a `title:`. `Sidebar.Item` takes an `icon:` too.\n\nThese are the 72 built-in icons:\n\n- **General**: `home`, `menu`, `search`, `close`, `user`, `settings`, `check`, `plus`, `minus`, `edit`, `trash`, `star`, `heart`, `mail`, `bell`, `download`, `upload`, `eye`, `link`, `calendar`, `filter`, `info`, `warning`, `arrow-left`, `arrow-right`, `chevron-down`, `chevron-right`, `chevron-left`, `logout`, `copy`, `sun`, `moon`.\n- **For an editor or a tool**: `folder`, `folder-plus`, `folder-off`, `file`, `file-plus`, `refresh`, `alert-circle`, `clock`, `dot`, `diff`, `book`, `git-pull-request`, `pin`, `git-branch`, `more-horizontal`, `window-minimize`, `window-maximize`, `outline`, `help-circle`, `collapse-all`, `locate`, `chevron-up`, `globe`, `external-link`, `panel-down`, `panel-up`, `code`, `monitor`, `git-commit`, `play`, `layers`, `insert`, `grid`, `list`, `square`, `pill`, `image`, `sparkle`, `lock`, `type`, `pointer`, `reach`, `frame`, `line`, `pipette`, `comment`, `hand`, `zoom`, `ruler`, `eye-off`, `unlock`.\n\nA name that is not in the list draws as the word itself, and the compiler warns with `V08`.")

        Callout("The build keeps only the glyphs your program names. If an icon's name arrives at run time, set `\"build\": \{ \"runtime\": \"full\" \}` so every glyph ships.", kind: .important)

        Markdown("For an icon that is not built in, put an SVG file in `public/` and show it with `Image(src: \"/icons/rocket.svg\", alt: \"\")`.")

        Heading("Show a carousel", id: "show-a-carousel").h2

        Markdown("To show slides one at a time, wrap each in a `Carousel.Slide` inside a `Carousel`. This page shows three photographs that advance every five seconds:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Gallery(path: "/", title: "Gallery", description: "Three pictures.") {
    Heading("Gallery").h1
    Carousel(label: "Recent work", autoplay: true, interval: 5000) {
        Carousel.Slide { Image(src: "/one.jpg", alt: "A bridge at dusk") }
        Carousel.Slide { Image(src: "/two.jpg", alt: "The harbour from above") }
        Carousel.Slide { Image(src: "/three.jpg", alt: "Rain on the market") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A carousel has a named previous button, a named next button, and a dot per slide. Its `label:` names the region for screen readers. It is a `region` described as a carousel, unless you set its `role:` or `aria-roledescription:` yourself.\n\nWith `autoplay`, the carousel also has a button that stops it. It pauses while the pointer or the keyboard focus is on it, and it does not advance at all for a reader who asked for reduced motion.")

        Heading("Show another page in a frame", id: "show-another-page-in-a-frame").h2

        Markdown("To show another page inside yours (a video player, a map, a preview your app builds), use `Frame`. Give it a `title:` that says what it shows: a screen reader announces a frame by its title, and a frame without one draws an `A17` warning.\n\nThis page embeds a video player and a preview of the site's own, and shows what the preview sends back:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Talk(path: "/", title: "The talk", description: "The launch talk, and what the preview says.") {
    state heard = ""
    Heading("The talk").h1
    Frame(src: "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/dQw4w9WgXcQ", title: "The launch talk", allow: "fullscreen")
    Frame(src: "/preview/", title: "Preview", sandbox: "allow-scripts") {
        on message(m) { heard = m.data }
    }
    Text(heard)
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- `on message(m)` hears what the framed page posts with `window.parent.postMessage(…)`, and nothing that any other page posts. `m.data` is what it sent.\n- `sandbox:` limits what the framed page may do: `\"allow-scripts allow-forms\"` allows scripts and forms, and an empty string allows nothing.\n- `allow:` names the browser features the framed page may use.\n\nA frame from another origin must be named in `meta.frame`, or the page's Content-Security-Policy refuses it ([Security](/docs/guide/security)).")

        Heading("Draw HTML you give the frame", id: "draw-html-you-give-the-frame").h3

        Markdown("To draw HTML instead of loading an address (an example a page renders, or a preview of what a reader is writing), use `srcdoc:`. The frame draws it sandboxed as `sandbox:` says, so `sandbox: \"\"` draws it with no script and no reach into your page. A value that reads state draws the new HTML each time it changes, and the pre-rendered page has the HTML before any script runs.\n\nThis page previews a note as the reader types it:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Preview(path: "/preview-note", title: "Preview", description: "A note, drawn as it will look.") {
    state note = "Hello"
    Heading("Preview").h1
    Input(bind: note, label: "Note")
    Frame(srcdoc: "<p>{note}</p>", title: "The note, drawn", sandbox: "")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Frame your own pages", id: "frame-your-own-pages").h3

        Markdown("A page of your own site (`src: \"/preview/\"`) needs no extra setting. The site's pages are served refusing to be framed by anyone (`X-Frame-Options: DENY`). A project that places a `Frame` with a site-relative `src:` is served `SAMEORIGIN` instead, both by `wf serve` and in the `_headers` file a build writes. Your site may frame its own pages, and no other site may.")

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Table(caption: "Troubleshooting") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("What you see").header
                    Table.Cell("Why, and what to do").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The page jumps when a picture arrives")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The picture is an SVG, a URL or a path built at run time, which the build cannot measure. Give it `width:` and `height:`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("An icon shows as a word")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The name is not one of the built-in icons (`V08`), or it arrives at run time and the build left its glyph out. Use a listed name, or set `build.runtime` to `\"full\"`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A09` on a `Video` or `Audio`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Add `captions:` to the video or `transcript:` to the recording") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A17` on a `Frame`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Add a `title:` that says what the frame shows") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A frame from another site stays blank")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Its origin is not in `meta.frame`, so the policy blocks it") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf assets --plan` lists widths it will not write") }
                    Table.Cell("Those files would save nothing; the original serves at those widths")
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
